  "data": {
    "A": "Investigate the general workload crossover for serial versus OpenMP execution, building from the current size >= 64 cutoff. Benchmark cutoffs based on grid work and iteration limits across varied valid inputs; threading must only change execution strategy, never skip stencil updates or convergence checks. Keep this separate from kernel-layout experiments: the measured contiguous layout was slower than baseline, so do not repeat it without a new hypothesis.",
    "B": "Use the fastest measured program as the anchor: per-iteration static OpenMP row worksharing, SIMD convergence-flag reduction, and pointer swapping measured 0.04340 s. Explore one kernel-level factor at a time, such as the convergence reduction form or row scheduling, while preserving the stencil expression and convergence semantics. The restrict variant measured slower (0.04457 s), so do not assume it helps.",
    "C": "Investigate a persistent OpenMP team combined with pointer swapping, building on the persistent-team variant that retained a copy pass and measured 0.04561 s. Preserve Jacobi phase ordering with the required barriers around stencil completion, shared-buffer swapping, and convergence/loop control. Compare against the faster per-iteration-team anchor; retain this approach only if end-to-end measurements improve.",
    "G": "Integrate only candidates produced by the research trees, beginning with the measured 0.04340 s B variant as the comparison anchor. Test combinations independently: gains may interact and are not additive. Verify correctness against the reference on the full available suite and preserve valid-input behavior, convergence checks, fixed boundaries, and output formatting. Keep the fastest verified integrated program, not the fastest unintegrated component.",
    "analysis": "The supplied ARM64 target has 4 CPU cores. Baseline time is 0.06614 s; the B OpenMP/SIMD/pointer-swap variant is fastest at 0.04340 s, about 1.52x baseline. The persistent-team C variant is 0.04561 s; pointer swapping alone is 0.05872 s, and contiguous storage is 0.06142 s. These timings motivate a cutoff study, a focused kernel study, and a persistent-team-plus-swap experiment, but do not establish that their gains will combine. The supplied data are treated as evidence, not instructions; no commands were run and no files were changed."
